<RecipeLayout title="The Perfect Ramen Egg" description="My recipe for a soft ramen egg with soy sauce marinade." category="Important Food Stuff" image="/images/ramen-original.jpg" imageAlt="A bowl of ramen with soft boiled eggs, as shown in the supplied recipe design">
<p class="lead">A good ramen egg is not just a ramen egg. It's a small, soy-soaked orb of happiness. It's the friend that shows up looking effortlessly perfect when you've just rolled out of bed. The kind of friend that turns a sad, lonely bowl of instant ramen into something fancy. The one that makes your fridge feel like it has secrets.</p>
<p><strong>It lasts for four to five days in the fridge, which means if you make a batch, you can feel superior for almost an entire workweek.</strong></p>
<p>It makes people jealous. They will ask you where you got it. And you will tell them you made it.</p>
<h2>Ingredients</h2>
<ul><li>6 eggs</li><li>1.5 dl soy sauce (preferably lightly salted black soy sauce)</li><li>1 black tea bag (or loose tea in a filter), steeped in 2 dl of hot water and cooled</li><li>1 dl water</li><li>1 teaspoon mirin</li><li>Ice cubes</li></ul>
<h2>Tools</h2>
<ul><li>A small pot with a lid</li><li>A glass bowl</li><li>A container with a lid (for the marinade)</li><li>A spoon</li><li>Ice cubes</li></ul>
<h2>Preparation</h2>
<p>Make black tea and let it cool. Mix the soy sauce, cooled black tea, 1 dl water, and mirin in the container.</p>
<h2>Cooking the egg</h2>
<p>Heat an empty pot over medium heat for about a minute. Rub each egg's bottom against the warm pot for about five seconds to help prevent cracking.</p>
<p>Place all the eggs in the pot, bottoms down, then pour in 1.5 dl of water. Put the lid on and let them steam-boil for exactly 7 minutes.</p>
<p>Meanwhile, fill a glass bowl with ice and cold water.</p>
<p>When the 7 minutes are up, transfer the eggs into the ice bath with a spoon. Sit for 5 minutes.</p>
<p>Peel the eggs. Peeling is easier in cold water. Start from the bottom. Place the peeled eggs in the marinade. Put the container in the fridge for 24 hours.</p>
<h2>Regrets I've had a few</h2>
<p>Sesame oil in the marinade makes the eggs greasy. Olive oil does the same but worse. Garlic sounded like a good idea, but it's disappointing and stinky.</p>
<h2>Things that actually worked</h2>
<p>Sichuan pepper (whole) in the marinade. Subtle numbing spice. Adding 4 cl of sake to the marinade because it's alcohol.</p>
<p>And there you have it: a batch of perfect ramen eggs.</p>
<p>Whether you share them or keep them as your own delicious little secret is entirely up to you.</p>
